// @check
// @error no field 'toString' on {s: f64} — the `toString` in scope takes Circle (the body of `describeU` at the call's argument types)
// @hint redundant type annotation: `toString` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `describeU` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// The `: string` return annotations below are DECLARED rather than deleted: a
// fixture for a type feature should read as the documentation it is, and the
// checker calling them redundant is a fact about inference, not about the example.
//
// THE UNBOUNDED SPELLING of the hole `error-bound-unsatisfied-at-call.vl` pins for
// a BOUNDED parameter (silent-class-inventory D1005), and it is a SOUNDNESS fix.
//
// `ufcsCallTy` gates dispatch on `assignable(recvTy, params[0])`, and `assignable`
// answers TRUE for a `TyVar` on EITHER side — so inside a generic body the
// `self`-function is selected by NAME ALONE and the receiver's FIT is never
// re-asked at the pin. Both instantiations below therefore passed `vl check`, and
// the second one — whose `{s: f64}` no free `toString` can take — reached the
// emitter as INVALID WASM (measured on the 2026-09-01 seed).
//
// THE MESSAGE IS THE DIRECT SPELLING'S, plus the contract. `const v = {s: 2.0}`
// followed by `v.toString()` is `no field 'toString' on {s: f64}`, and the two
// spellings are one annotation apart, so they are one sentence apart
// (`memCstrTy`'s header). What the pin adds is WHAT THE RECEIVER MISSED — the
// `self` parameter's own type — because at the call site the offending type and
// the contract it failed are both visible and neither alone is actionable.
//
// THE FIRST INSTANTIATION IS LEGAL AND IS NOT WHAT IS REPORTED: `{r: 1.0}` fits
// `Circle` and prints `<C>` on its own (see `unbounded-type-param-unchanged.vl`,
// the refutation pin that keeps that half working). Exactly one error here.
type Circle = { r: f64 }

function toString(self: Circle): string {
  if self.r > 0.0 { return "C" }
  "c"
}

function describeU<T>(x: T): string { "<" + x.toString() + ">" }

print(describeU({ r: 1.0 }))
print(describeU({ s: 2.0 }))
